{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012X1b11yX5Vrin4E2gSV6xe","type":"tool_result","content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:318:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:783:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:390:Now the five components of the control plane. The control plane's components make global decisions about the cluster — scheduling, for example — as well as detecting and responding to cluster events, such as starting up a new Pod when a controller's declared replica count is unsatisfied [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]. A Pod, for now, is the unit Kubernetes schedules and runs — Chapter 5 is its whole subject.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:445:A PodSpec, for now, is simply the description of what containers should run. Chapter 4 gives you `spec` in general; Chapter 5 gives the PodSpec its proper treatment.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:447:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 — Pods, PodSpecs, and what \"running and healthy\" means precisely]*\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:111:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:269:*(Deployment is Chapter 6's resource and the instances it manages are Chapter 5's. Take the example for its shape — a number in a spec, a reality in a status, a correction — and meet the resource properly later. [cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §1 — Deployments and ReplicaSets])*\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:273:That is the most reused sentence in this book. Chapter 5 reads it against a Pod's phase, Chapter 6 reads it against a replica count, Chapter 13 reads it as the first thing you check when something is wrong. Learn it in that exact shape.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:531:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:1186:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:1192:Chapter 5 introduces the disposable thing. Chapter 6 introduces what holds the intent.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:102:| Requests/limits precede scheduling and OOMKilled | Introduced Ch 5, applied Ch 7, diagnosed Ch 13 |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:110:| ServiceAccount needed by RBAC subjects and Secret types | Planted in Ch 5 as Pod identity; full treatment Ch 12 |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:159:| Ch 5 Pods | G3 (requests, limits, QoS classes), G7 (ServiceAccounts) |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"435c9759-7e38-4588-98f9-531a8ae494c6","uuid":"8109126e-1031-435f-840f-fe32b5ab1bcc","timestamp":"2026-08-24T10:06:18.332Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:318:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-02-cargo-in-standard-crates.md:783:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:390:Now the five components of the control plane. The control plane's components make global decisions about the cluster — scheduling, for example — as well as detecting and responding to cluster events, such as starting up a new Pod when a controller's declared replica count is unsatisfied [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]. A Pod, for now, is the unit Kubernetes schedules and runs — Chapter 5 is its whole subject.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:445:A PodSpec, for now, is simply the description of what containers should run. Chapter 4 gives you `spec` in general; Chapter 5 gives the PodSpec its proper treatment.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:447:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 — Pods, PodSpecs, and what \"running and healthy\" means precisely]*\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:111:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:269:*(Deployment is Chapter 6's resource and the instances it manages are Chapter 5's. Take the example for its shape — a number in a spec, a reality in a status, a correction — and meet the resource properly later. [cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §1 — Deployments and ReplicaSets])*\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:273:That is the most reused sentence in this book. Chapter 5 reads it against a Pod's phase, Chapter 6 reads it against a replica count, Chapter 13 reads it as the first thing you check when something is wrong. Learn it in that exact shape.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:531:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:1186:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:1192:Chapter 5 introduces the disposable thing. Chapter 6 introduces what holds the intent.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:102:| Requests/limits precede scheduling and OOMKilled | Introduced Ch 5, applied Ch 7, diagnosed Ch 13 |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:110:| ServiceAccount needed by RBAC subjects and Secret types | Planted in Ch 5 as Pod identity; full treatment Ch 12 |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:159:| Ch 5 Pods | G3 (requests, limits, QoS classes), G7 (ServiceAccounts) |","numLines":14,"totalLines":14}}
